  "body": "I propose to remove res.json() requirement with a backwards compatibility.\r\n\r\nFetch API already knows the content type of response and response is application/json shouldn't require an extra line `.then(res => res.json())` but do it by default. For backwards compatibility even if no longer res.json() required but is still defined, don't fall into exception, make like res.json() returning res\r\n\r\nI think almost 100% usage of Fetch API is waiting for JSON response, and requiring `.then(res => res.json())` feels unnecessary.\r\n\r\nRef https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#fetch-api\r\n```\r\nfetch(\"https://pk.example/berlin-calling.json\", {mode:\"cors\"})\r\n  .then(res => {\r\n    if(res.headers.get(\"content-type\") &&\r\n       res.headers.get(\"content-type\").toLowerCase().indexOf(\"application/json\") >= 0) {\r\n      return res.json()\r\n    } else {\r\n      throw new TypeError()\r\n    }\r\n  }).then(processJSON)\r\n```\r\n\r\ninsted could be\r\n```\r\nfetch(\"https://pk.example/berlin-calling.json\", {mode:\"cors\"}).then(processJSON)\r\n```\r\nresulting the same result.\r\n\r\nThe only consideration I see is what would happen if by mistake the response returns a HTML page with status 200. If I try to process inside fetch API then it should still fall into catch(). Maybe it would be great to have additional option e.g. `{verifyJSON: true}` to force to execute res.json() without defining it. In this way if content-type will differ it can succeed to convert to JSON or will fall into correct TypeError",
